Client architecture
===================

Basic elements:

- Tcp Client. Handles the connection to the server. Can send to it, and calls callbacks at reception and connection closing. It has no blocking call (timeout instead).
- Supervisor. Analyses commands from server, and applies them.
- Client manager. Same as in server but has no socket information, so they are referenced only by nickname.
- Table. Same as in server, with an additional drawing method, and a coordinate to id converter. Also has a local zoom + position, for the displaying.
- Event handler. Records user input (keyboard and mouse) and calls appropriate callbacks. These can either change local game status or send requests to the server. It also takes care of tracking the hot elements (at global level, to allow drag and dropping between UI elements, for instance).
- Drawer. Handles the loading of packs and the resolving of sprite name -> actual sprite. Has the graphical part to make it appear on screen, and the event handling that goes with it.
- Contextual menu. It's a singleton object, since only one can appear at one time. It is actually always there, but only visible when activated. Any object can activate the menu and fill it with elements associated to callbacks.
- Text Input box. Same as menu, there is only one, it is always there, and only activated when needed.

The main loop is never blocked, since we need constant refreshing and input possibility.

All these classes are used as singleton, so the global name of the class is reused for the instance.

Some of these classes are copied and modified from the server ones, while some are actually the same (from directory "common"), with additional methods in another file (from main source directory). Some objects need a second init() method, called initview(), to be called after the original init().

x. Dealing with delay
---------------------

All actions that modify game state are delayed by the round trip to the server. Moving an object with the mouse really sends a request, and the movement occurs only when receiving the answer from the server.

To minimize traffic and delay, only final positions/angles/zoom/etc. are sent as requests to the server (not all intermediary positions occupied during drap & drop or such). Upon reception of the server answer, the game state is updated, and objects are smoothly interpolated to their final positions.

1. Tcp Client
-------------

Nothing special, since it deals only with one socket.

2. Client manager
-----------------

Identical to the server's one, only socket information is not given.

3. Table
--------

Follows closely the server's model. Indexed by game entity id, as given by the server. A draw method takes care of forwarding to all game entities, taking in account the general viewing zoom/position. It must be able to know in which object happened a click. Game objects are built upon server game objects, see additions later.
Has a coordinateS converter between game world and screen (to be used with id/coord conversion).

4. Event handler
----------------

The tough part, especially since the UI actions have not been decided yet. Will include:
- drag & drop to move objects
- drag on the table to move viewing position
- wheel up and down to change viewing zoom
- right click (or ctrl-click) for contextual menu

All objects interested in input events register to the Event Handler, who forwards them.
It also takes care of keeping track of the hot object (the one under the mouse).

5. Game entities
----------------

Are basically the same as server's ones, with added methods in another source.

- immediate position and angle, which serves for interpolation. They are the one really drawn. At each update they go closer to the real ones, which are given by the server.
- dragged/rotated flag, with initial relative click position. 

- draw at immediate position/angle.
- inside coordinates test: based on rectangular shapes with the immediate position/angle/scales.

6. Protocol: server to client
-----------------------------

It is closely based on the client to server protocol. Descriptions are omitted when they are identical to those in the latter.

x. Connection
-------------

When a client handshakes and gets clearance 0, it is revealed to the others.

CLIENT nick clearance r g b

The command YOUARE is sent with the same parameters to the origin of the handshake.
The command THEREIS is sent upon connection, for each client already connected, with same parameters as CLIENT.

xx. Errors
----------

The server can answer to a request that fails by:

ERROR message

[We might need in the future to have specific error messages or codes, for better interpretation by the client.]

a. User data and chatting
-------------------------

NICK oldnick newnick
COLOR nick r g b
SAY nick message
TELL source dest [message]	(message is sent only to the target)
QUIT nick
LOST nick	(if nick's connection drops)
PROMOTE nick target level	(nick can be "<server>" if the promotion was automatic)
KICK nick target
PONG time	(time is the server time at reception of the PING. This is sent only to the sender of PING)

b. Manipulation of game entities
--------------------------------

ENTITY nick id
MOVE nick id x y
ROTATE nick id a	
SCALE nick id s1 [s2]
FLIP nick id new	(new indicates the new top)
DELETE nick id
MOVEZ nick id pos num	(num is the number of elements that had to be moved up by one.
	Since client might not have all info about entities, this is necessary)

c. Basic entities
-----------------

IMAGE nick id N sprite1 [sprite2 ...]
SETCOLOR nick id r g b
LABEL nick id text text2
SETLABEL nick id text
ATTACH nick id [target]
